# Rudolf OS 1.5.9 (docs/plan-1.5.9.md 15.5): everything on the computer that listens, who may use it and how it checks.
# rudolf-selftest's "listeners" line fails when a TCP port is listened on, or a Unix socket accepts connections, that a
# line here does not name; qt/host/listeners59_t.sh fails when the sources start a listener this list does not name.
# Columns, separated by tabs: KIND (tcp, tcp6, udp, unix) WHERE (address:port or a path; * matches anything in a
# name, @ is an abstract socket) PROGRAM (as the sources name it) WHEN, WHO may use it, and HOW it checks.
tcp	127.0.0.1:8080	rudolfd	always	the desktop's processes (its memory group) and root; /lib/ /pkg/ /dlf/ and the page's files: every program on the computer	the page and /api/ only for the desktop's memory group (15.1) or root, /api/ with the per-boot token too; loopback only
tcp	127.0.0.1:8099	llama-server	while Blitzen's model is loaded (rudolf-ai)	rudolfd	runs as the "ai" user; answers only requests with the random key in /run/rudolf/ai/key (root only), which rudolfd adds; loopback only
tcp	0.0.0.0:8443	rudolf-remote	only while remote access is on (Settings › Remote access)	people on the network with the pairing code, then a Rudolf OS account	TLS (the computer's own certificate); the 6-digit pairing code once per browser, an account's password (PBKDF2), five wrong tries lock it a minute; the person at the computer approves unless the account may connect unattended; the firewall
tcp6	:::8443	rudolf-remote	only while remote access is on	as above	as above
tcp	127.0.0.1:9050	tor	only while a Tor window is open in Oracle (rudolfd starts it, 1.5.9 16.1)	the Tor windows' Chromium (SOCKS5); any program on this computer could	none beyond loopback (it is a SOCKS proxy to the Tor network, as Tor Browser's own); runs as the "tor" user, client only, its files in memory
tcp	127.0.0.1:9222	chromium	only on a test disc (rudolf.debug=1): never on a release	root's test scripts (cdp-eval)	none beyond loopback: a test disc is for testing only
tcp	*:53	dnsmasq	only while Share this connection is on (the hotspot's interface)	devices joined to the hotspot	WPA2/WPA3 with the hotspot's password; --bind-interfaces, not loopback
tcp6	*:53	dnsmasq	only while Share this connection is on	devices joined to the hotspot	as above
udp	*:53	dnsmasq	only while Share this connection is on	devices joined to the hotspot	as above
udp	*:67	dnsmasq	only while Share this connection is on (DHCP)	devices joined to the hotspot	as above
unix	@/tmp/.X11-unix/X0	Xorg	always (the display)	root, the desktop's user and the signed-in account's own Linux user (15.3)	X authorization (1.5.9): a cookie at each start, root's only (-auth /run/rudolf/xauth), and the display given by user (X's localuser, the socket's peer): rudolf-display --xallow, by the session and rudolfd at sign-in, --xdeny at sign-out
unix	/tmp/.X11-unix/X0	Xorg	always (the display)	as above	as above (Xvfb on the software display: the same socket)
unix	/run/dbus/system_bus_socket	dbus-daemon	while Bluetooth or the modem service runs	every program on the computer	D-Bus's own policy (/etc/rudolf/dbus-system.conf): BlueZ, bluealsa and ModemManager only, method calls checked by the bus
unix	/run/user/*/bus	dbus-daemon	the session (signed in)	the desktop user's programs	the socket is the user's (0700 runtime folder); D-Bus checks the user (EXTERNAL)
unix	/run/user/*/pulse/native	pulseaudio	the session	the desktop user's programs	the runtime folder is the user's (0700); auth-anonymous inside it
unix	/run/user/*/keyring/*	gnome-keyring-daemon	the signed-in account's session (1.6.0 Part 14: its keyring, the Secret Service)	that account's programs	the runtime folder is the account's user's (0700); the Secret Service itself is on the account's session bus
unix	/run/rudolf/pulse/native	pulseaudio	the session (15.3)	the accounts' own Linux users (the audio group)	the folder is rudolf:audio 0750; auth-anonymous inside it
unix	/run/user/*/.flatpak-helper/*	flatpak	while a Flatpak app runs	that app's sandbox	Flatpak's own
unix	/run/user/*/.dbus-proxy/*	xdg-dbus-proxy	while a Flatpak app runs	that app's sandbox	filters the app's D-Bus calls (Flatpak's permissions)
unix	/run/user/*/doc/*	xdg-document-portal	the session	the desktop user's programs	Flatpak's document portal
unix	/run/rudolf/term/*.sock	rudolf-debian	while a Debian terminal is open	rudolfd	/run/rudolf/term is 0700 root
unix	/run/rudolf/wpa/*	wpa_supplicant	while Wi-Fi is on	root (rudolfd, wifi.sh)	the control folder is root's
unix	/run/rudolf/hotspot/ctrl/*	hostapd	while Share this connection is on	root	the control folder is root's
unix	/tmp/.org.chromium.Chromium.*/SingletonSocket	chromium	while Chromium runs	the desktop user	Chromium's own: the folder is the user's (0700)
unix	/tmp/org.chromium.Chromium.*/SingletonSocket	chromium	while Chromium runs (its newer folder name, seen on 2026-11-14)	the desktop user	Chromium's own: the folder is the user's (0700)
unix	/tmp/.wine-*/server-*/socket	wineserver	while a Windows program runs	the program's own user	Wine's: the folder is the user's (0700)
unix	/run/user/*/wayland-*	waydroid	while Android apps run (Debian)	the desktop user's programs	the runtime folder is the user's (0700)
unix	/run/udev/control	systemd-udevd	always (1.6.0 Part 9: devices through udev)	root (udevadm: rudolf-init, the device scripts)	the socket is root's (0600 in a root folder); udevd takes control messages from root only (it checks the peer's credentials)
unix	/run/cups/cups.sock	rudolf-printq	always (1.6.1 Part 9: the print queue; started by rudolf-init)	every program on this computer: the desktop through rudolfd, Linux apps through CUPS's client library (Debian's /run/cups and Flatpak's cups socket are this one)	runs as the "print" user (no_new_privs); each job belongs to the user the kernel says sent it (SO_PEERCRED; root may say for whom: rudolfd, the signed-in account's user); only that user cancels it, other users' jobs are listed without their names; PDF only; no network port
tcp	127.0.0.1:600*	ipp-usb	while an IPP over USB printer is plugged in (the USB printers extension, 1.6.0 Part 8)	the print queue (the "print" user), root and the desktop's user	loopback only (its interface = loopback, no DNS-SD), ports 60000-60099; any other user's new connection to them dropped (nftables, the table inet rudolfprint that usbprint loads: 1.6.1 Part 9)
udp	*:5353	rudolf-print	only while Find printers runs (about 4 s: rudolfd's print/find, the print queue's search; 1.6.1 Part 9)	printers on the local network answering its multicast DNS question	multicast DNS only (224.0.0.251, joined on the interfaces that are up, never a cellular one); only responses from port 5353, arriving on a joined interface, not from this computer's own addresses; the firewall lets in only port 5353 from port 5353, to the group or from the same link (TTL 255), never on a cellular interface; read-only parsing (parse_mdns, bounded; fuzzed by qt/host/mdns161_t.sh); the group left and the port closed when find ends
tcp	0.0.0.0:47731	rudolf-duel	only while a player hosts a two-player Library game on the home network (1.6.2 Part 30: rudolfd's duel/host; it stops when its duel ends (the hosting game leaves, or either player once paired), within a minute if none hosts, after 10 minutes if nobody joins, or on duel/stop)	the two games on the local network (the hosting computer's own and the other player's)	runs as the signed-in account's own Linux user, never root (no new privileges); takes connections only from loopback and private addresses; the firewall's duel chain lets the port in from private addresses only, never on a cellular interface, and only while it runs; the Library relay's protocol: the room code, the same game and version, at most 4 connections, 8 KB a message, 90 a second, 10 s to say hello; addresses are never passed on
udp	*:5353	rudolf-duel	only while a player hosts a two-player Library game on the home network (as above), and for about 3 s while duel/find looks for one	computers on the local network asking for _emberduel._tcp (multicast DNS)	multicast DNS only (224.0.0.251, joined on the interfaces that are up, never a cellular one); answers only questions for its own service, instance or host, from private addresses arriving on a joined interface; find reads only responses from port 5353 on a joined interface (parsing bounded: every pointer and length checked; fuzzed by qt/host/duel162_t.sh); the group left and the port closed when it ends
